# provenance_corrections.jsonl — read by consonance/tools/essay-provenance.js
#
# A commit's body is prose, and prose can be wrong about what the commit did. When a commit is
# already pushed it cannot be amended, so the only repair is a second record. This is it.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS BELIEVED. Every row is checked against a blob FROZEN IN THE COMMIT IT CORRECTS
# (`git show <sha>:<evidence>`), and a row that does not pass is printed as REFUSED, never dropped:
#   1  <sha> must be a commit the tool read, and its diff must contain <path>
#   2  <evidence> must be readable at that sha
#   3  the evidence's header must carry <seat> on a line that also carries an authorship marker
#      (SEAT:, placed here by, written by, spawned by, filed by, authored by, committed by)
#   4  if <evidence> is a different file from <path>, its header must NAME <path>
# The first verified row for a (commit, path) stands; a later one is REFUSED as CONFLICTING. So a
# correction can only ever redirect attribution to what the artifact itself said when it landed —
# it cannot be satisfied after the fact by a plausible-looking pointer.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO: it authenticates against the frozen record, not against the world. A
# header that was false when it was written is inherited, not caught. A file with no header and
# nothing naming it cannot be corrected at all — that failure is closed and printed.
#
# Fields: sha, path, seat, evidence (optional, defaults to path), by, at, note (optional).
# Lines beginning with # are skipped. Append only; never edit a row that has stood.
#
